{
  "slug": "surprise-anesthesia-bill-no-surprises-act",
  "metaTitle": "Surprise Anesthesia Bill: What the No Surprises Act Covers",
  "metaDescription": "Most private plans get federal protection from a surprise anesthesia bill at in-network facilities. See what the No Surprises Act covers and how to check it.",
  "h1": "Surprise Anesthesia Bill: What the No Surprises Act Covers",
  "subtitle": "A coverage table built from federal text, plus the order to check a bill against your explanation of benefits.",
  "introText": [
    "In most cases, the [No Surprises Act](https://www.cms.gov/medical-bill-rights) protects you from a surprise anesthesia bill after planned care at an in-network facility. That covers an out-of-network anesthesiologist at an in-network hospital, hospital outpatient department or ambulatory surgical center, if you use most types of private health insurance. The [Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)](https://www.cms.gov/nosurprises) lists anesthesiology among 9 specialties that are not allowed to ask you to sign away that protection.",
    "Based on CMS pages we read in full on November 28, 2026, the protection has firm limits. Ground ambulances generally fall outside it. So do short-term limited duration plans, health care sharing ministries and fixed indemnity plans.",
    "A bill can still be correct. If the amount matches the Patient Balance on your explanation of benefits (EOB), it equals what CMS calls the amount you owe after your insurer has paid everything else. If the bill is higher, CMS points you to your provider, your plan and the No Surprises Help Desk at 1-800-985-3059."
  ],
  "inlineCta": {
    "text": "If your surprise anesthesia bill is higher than the Patient Balance on your EOB, the No Surprises Help Desk takes complaints online and at 1-800-985-3059.",
    "buttonLabel": "Submit a Complaint",
    "buttonHref": "https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/your-patient-rights/medical-bill-rights/get-help/submit-complaint"
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "billed-more-than-in-network",
      "heading": "Can You Be Billed More Than In-Network Rates?",
      "content": [
        "For emergency care, CMS states the limit outright: if your plan covers emergency care, you can't be charged more for emergency medical services than the in-network cost-sharing rate. That limit applies to the hospital, the providers treating you at the hospital, and an air ambulance provider that took you to the hospital. CMS defines cost-sharing as a copayment, deductible or coinsurance.",
        "For planned, non-emergency care, CMS uses different wording on its [rights page for people using insurance](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/your-patient-rights/medical-bill-rights/know-your-rights/insurance). In most cases, you're protected from unexpected out-of-network bills for non-emergency care related to a visit to an in-network hospital, hospital outpatient department or ambulatory surgical center. CMS gives the example of a local hospital that is in-network while a provider treating you there is out-of-network.",
        "An out-of-network anesthesiologist at an in-network surgery center fits that example. CMS names anesthesiology, radiology, pathology, and diagnostic and laboratory services among the specialties that are not allowed to ask you for a notice and consent form. Those providers can't get you to sign away the balance billing protections for that care.",
        "Your coverage type decides whether any of this applies. The protections cover insurance through an employer (including a Federal Employees Health Benefits plan), the federal Health Insurance Marketplace, a State-based Marketplace or other individual market coverage. The law took effect on January 1, 2022.",
        "CMS also notes that some health plans don't cover emergency care. If you're unsure what your plan covers, contact your insurance company before assuming the emergency limit applies.",
        "The disagreement over an out-of-network payment amount belongs to the provider and your plan. CMS describes its consumer protections as removing consumers from payment disagreements between providers, health care facilities and health plans. Its October 2021 rules set up an independent dispute resolution (IDR) process to settle those payment amounts between providers and plans."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "coverage-table",
      "heading": "What the No Surprises Act Covers and Does Not",
      "content": [
        "The No Surprises Act covers emergency care, air ambulance services and planned care at in-network hospitals, hospital outpatient departments and ambulatory surgical centers, and it leaves out ground ambulances and several plan types. The table is our synthesis of CMS text read in full on November 28, 2026. Each row names the CMS page it comes from.",
        "A yes for planned care can change if you signed a valid notice and consent form for a provider who is allowed to ask for one. The next section covers that exception.",
        "Two rows change the answer for an anesthesia bill. If your procedure happened in a doctor's office that is not a hospital outpatient department, the planned-care protection does not reach it. If the facility itself was out-of-network, the protection for planned care at in-network facilities does not apply either.",
        "Medicare, Medicaid, Indian Health Services, Veterans Affairs Health Care and TRICARE follow their own rules. CMS says only that these plans already protect you from some unexpected out-of-network bills. For a billing issue under one of them, CMS offers an [action plan tool](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/your-patient-rights/medical-bill-rights/get-help/find-action-plan) that asks a few questions about your bill."
      ],
