    {
      "id": "consent-form",
      "heading": "When Signing a Consent Form Gives Up Your Protection",
      "content": [
        "A notice and consent form is the document that can give up your No Surprises Act protection, and CMS heads that topic with the warning that signing one may increase your costs. CMS says an out-of-network provider may ask you to sign one for post-stabilization services after an emergency room visit. Signing it means you give up your billing protections for that care.",
        "For planned care at an in-network facility, a valid form can waive the protection for some scheduled care from an out-of-network provider. The form also can't be used when no in-network provider is available. A shortage of in-network providers bars the form.",
        "The answer flips for a provider outside the CMS list. Your primary surgeon does not appear on that list, while assistant surgeons do. If you signed a valid form for a provider who is allowed to ask, that provider's out-of-network bill can fall outside the protection.",
        "When someone hands you a form, look at who is asking. If the provider is on the list below, CMS says they are not allowed to ask, and if you want to keep your protection, don't sign. If you already signed one, keep the signed and dated copy, because CMS lists it among the documents to include with a complaint.",
        "CMS lists these specialties as not allowed to ask you for a notice and consent form:"
      ],
      "bullets": [
        "Anesthesiology",
        "Radiology",
        "Pathology",
        "Diagnostic and laboratory services",
        "Emergency medicine",
        "Neonatology",
        "Assistant surgeons",
        "Hospitalists",
        "Intensivists"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "viral-scripts",
      "heading": "What Viral Surprise Anesthesia Bill Scripts Get Wrong",
      "content": [
        "Viral surprise anesthesia bill scripts get the core protection right and the follow-up tools wrong. They tell patients to rip up their bills and mix up the rules for insured and uninsured patients.",
        "One post pushing the rip-it-up tactic passed 4,100 likes on November 28, 2026, and copycat step-by-step threads are spreading. No CMS page we read tells you to ignore, tear up or throw away a medical bill. The CMS pages tell you to compare the bill with your EOB, contact your plan and submit a complaint when the rules aren't followed.",
        "The good faith estimate is the first mix-up. CMS limits it to people who don't have health insurance or don't use it to pay for care. Usually, those patients get a written estimate when they schedule care at least 3 business days in advance or ask for one.",
        "An uninsured or self-pay patient may dispute a bill that is at least $400 more than the good faith estimate from that same provider, and the dispute has a 120-day window to start. Each estimate covers a single provider or facility, so CMS's surgery example calls for 2 estimates, one from the surgeon and one from the hospital. There is no estimate during emergency care.",
        "If you used insurance, the $400 dispute is the wrong tool. Your route runs through the EOB, your plan and the No Surprises Help Desk.",
        "Ground ambulances are the other mix-up. CMS says ground ambulance services generally aren't covered by the Act's billing protections unless a state law has different rules. A script that promises federal relief on a ground ambulance bill gets this wrong.",
        "Some scripts also overstate what an insured patient can refuse. You can contest a charge above the protected amount, and a bill that matches your EOB Patient Balance is the amount CMS says you owe after your insurer pays."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "check-steps",
      "heading": "How to Check a Surprise Anesthesia Bill Step by Step",
      "content": [
        "Check a surprise anesthesia bill by matching it against your EOB, calling your plan, and filing a complaint if the numbers still don't match. The steps follow CMS guidance, and the one practical step we added is marked as ours.",
        "An EOB isn't a bill. It shows Provider Charges, the amount your provider bills, and Allowed Charges, the amount your provider will be paid. It also shows Paid by Insurer and the Patient Balance, which CMS calls the amount you owe after your insurer has paid everything else.",
        "The EOB shows what you owe, and it does not show whether you already paid part of it. A remark code of 2 or 3 letters and numbers explains costs, and the bottom of the EOB describes each code.",
        "The online complaint form resets after 15 minutes of inactivity, so photograph your documents before you start. You can return later with your confirmation number to add more documents. CMS also points to state Consumer Assistance Programs and patient advocates on its [page about checking a bill for errors](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/your-patient-rights/medical-bill-rights/get-help/guides-resources/check-your-bill-errors).",
        "If the bill matches the Patient Balance, the federal protection has done its work, and the remaining amount is the share your plan assigned to you. Our guide to [payment plans and hospital financial assistance](/guides/how-to-deal-with-medical-debt) covers a balance you can't afford. If you pay from a health savings account (HSA) or flexible spending account (FSA), the [HSA and FSA reimbursement checklist](/guides/hsa-fsa-reimbursement-checklist) lists the records to keep.",
        "Work through these steps in order:"
      ],
      "bullets": [
        "Ask the provider's billing department for a detailed bill that lists the cost of each medical item or service.",
        "Get your EOB and find the Patient Balance. CMS says on its [EOB guide](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/your-patient-rights/medical-bill-rights/get-help/guides-resources/read-explanation-benefits) that your bill should not be higher than the Patient Balance, and if it is, talk to your provider.",
        "Compare the bill with your medical records and look for double billing, which CMS says is especially common when more than one provider treated you. You shouldn't get a bill for anything not documented in your records, and some providers charge a records fee.",
        "Call your plan at the phone number printed on the EOB. CMS says your insurance company might be able to fix billing errors with your provider.",
        "Ask the plan to review the claim as care covered by No Surprises Act protections. This is a practical step we suggest, and CMS itself says only to contact your plan.",
        "Gather your documents: the medical bill, a photo of your insurance card and the EOB, plus any signed notice and consent form, letters with the provider, appeal decisions and evidence of coverage.",
        "Submit a complaint to the No Surprises Help Desk online or at 1-800-985-3059, and write down your confirmation number. If the Help Desk needs more information, it will contact you within 60 days. Call 1-800-985-3059 with your confirmation number to check status."
      ],
      "orderedList": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ground-ambulance",
      "heading": "Ground Ambulance Bills and State Rules",
      "content": [
        "Ground ambulance bills generally fall outside the No Surprises Act, so ground ambulance providers are still allowed to charge out-of-network rates and cost-sharing unless state law says otherwise. CMS's [ground ambulance action plan](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/your-patient-rights/medical-bill-rights/get-help/find-action-plan/ground-ambulance-bill) applies if you got an unexpected bill, used health insurance and were taken to the hospital by ground ambulance.",
        "If you were billed in one of the states listed below, CMS suggests calling the ground ambulance provider. Tell them you're aware of surprise billing rules for ground ambulance services, because they may be able to reduce your bill.",
        "Outside those states, ask for a lower bill or a payment plan. CMS says providers or their billing departments can sometimes lower the price and may offer payment plans.",
        "The state list is dated November 15, 2021, and current rules in your state may differ. A Consumer Assistance Program in the state where you got care may be able to give advice for your specific case, and CMS links to those programs from the same ground ambulance action plan. Patient advocates and a friend or family member who can speak for you are the other help CMS lists.",
        "CMS lists these 10 states with ground ambulance billing protections as of November 15, 2021:"
      ],
      "bullets": [
        "Colorado",
        "Delaware",
        "Florida",
        "Illinois",
        "Maine",
        "Maryland",
        "New York",
        "Ohio",
        "Vermont",
        "West Virginia"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": {
    "heading": "Our Takeaway",
    "paragraphs": [
      "If you have private insurance and an out-of-network anesthesiologist, radiologist, pathologist or lab billed you after care at an in-network hospital, hospital outpatient department or ambulatory surgical center, the No Surprises Act generally protects you. Those specialties are not allowed to ask you to waive that protection. Compare the bill with the Patient Balance on your EOB, call your plan, and file with the No Surprises Help Desk at 1-800-985-3059 if the bill is higher.",
      "Based on CMS pages we read in full on November 28, 2026, this is a reported synthesis of federal guidance for most private plans. It is not a test of any provider or plan.",
      "If you're uninsured or paying yourself, use a good faith estimate and the $400 dispute instead of the EOB route. If the bill came from a ground ambulance, a doctor's office that is not a hospital outpatient department, an out-of-network facility, or a short-term, sharing ministry or fixed indemnity plan, the federal protection does not cover it. In that case, ask the provider for a lower bill or a payment plan."
    ],
    "conditions": "Applies to people using most private health insurance: employer plans including Federal Employees Health Benefits plans, the federal Health Insurance Marketplace, State-based Marketplaces and other individual market coverage. Planned-care protection covers care related to a visit to an in-network hospital, hospital outpatient department or ambulatory surgical center. The explicit in-network cost-sharing limit is CMS wording for emergency care only. Medicare, Medicaid, Indian Health Services, Veterans Affairs Health Care and TRICARE already protect you from some unexpected out-of-network bills under their own rules.",
    "whatWouldChange": "The answer changes if CMS or Congress revises the covered settings or the notice and consent exceptions, if you signed a valid notice and consent form for a provider allowed to ask for one, if your EOB shows the billed amount equals your Patient Balance, if a state ground ambulance law covers the bill, or if you did not use insurance, which moves you to the good faith estimate route."
  },
